# The crate

A package is a Cargo crate with three additions: a name that says so, a
library type `hql` can load, and a table naming the package. This is the
whole `Cargo.toml` of `hello`:

```toml
[package]
name = "hql-ext-hello"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2024"

[lib]
crate-type = ["cdylib", "rlib"]

[dependencies]
hql = "0.7"

[package.metadata.hql]
package = "hello"
provides = ["hello"]
modules = "modules"
```

## The name

The crate is named `hql-ext-` and then the package's name. The package
name is one lowercase word, and it is also the namespace of the package's
modules: `hello` provides `hello` and may provide `hello.words`, and
nothing outside that prefix. A package named `ml` provides `ml` and
`ml.metrics`.

## The library type

`crate-type` states what the compiler produces from the crate.

| Type | What it is | Why it is listed |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `cdylib` | a dynamic library: one file the operating system can load into a running process | this is what `hql` loads |
| `rlib` | a Rust library other Rust code links against at build time | so `cargo test` can test the crate's own functions |

Without `cdylib` the build leaves no file to load, and `hql package
install` says so. [[35-how-a-library-is-loaded/static-and-dynamic|Chapter 35]] explains the two kinds.

## The `hql` dependency

The crate depends on `hql`, the same crate the `hql` binary is built from,
and uses one module of it, `hql::ext`. `hql = "0.7"` is a range: every
0.7 release. It says which versions the crate's source is written for.

The version the crate is actually built against is not chosen by this
line. `hql package install` builds the crate against the exact version of
the `hql` that runs the command, and refuses a crate whose range excludes
that version before it builds anything
([[35-how-a-library-is-loaded/the-version-rule|the version rule]]).

## The package table

`[package.metadata.hql]` is the part Cargo ignores and `hql` reads.

| Key | What it states |
| --- | --- |
| `package` | the package's name, which is also its extension's name and its directory once installed |
| `provides` | every module the package provides, in the order they are loaded; a module that imports another comes after it |
| `modules` | the module root: the directory holding the contracts, relative to the crate |

`provides` is stated twice more, by the files under the module root and by
the extension in `src/lib.rs`. The three must agree, and a package where
they differ is refused when it is loaded.

A module's contract is found under the module root by its name, a dot
becoming a directory:

| Module | File |
| --- | --- |
| `hello` | `modules/hello/mod.hql` or `modules/hello.hql` |
| `hello.words` | `modules/hello/words.hql` or `modules/hello/words/mod.hql` |
